Got it 👍 You’re using JasperReports Community Edition (Jaspersoft Studio) and want conditional decimal formatting:

INR → 2 decimals

Non-INR → 4 decimals


This can be done purely with expressions, no paid features needed.


---

✅ Best & Clean Approach (Using Text Field Expression)

Assumptions

currency → java.lang.String (e.g. "INR", "USD")

balance → java.math.BigDecimal (or Double)



---

🔹 Step-by-Step in Jaspersoft Studio

Step 1: Select the Balance Text Field

Click on the Balance column text field in your report.



---

Step 2: Open Expression Editor

Right-click → Edit Expression

OR open Properties → Text Field → Expression



---

Step 3: Use This Expression

If balance is BigDecimal (recommended)

$F{currency}.equals("INR") 
? new java.text.DecimalFormat("#,##0.00").format($F{balance}) 
: new java.text.DecimalFormat("#,##0.0000").format($F{balance})


---

If balance is Double

$F{currency}.equals("INR") 
? new java.text.DecimalFormat("#,##0.00").format($F{balance}.doubleValue()) 
: new java.text.DecimalFormat("#,##0.0000").format($F{balance}.doubleValue())


---

Step 4: Change Text Field Type

Very important 👇

Select the text field

Go to Properties

Set Text Field Expression Class to:


java.lang.String

(Formatting returns String)


---

🔹 Alternative (Using Pattern Property – Less Flexible ❌)

You cannot dynamically change the pattern directly in the Pattern field in Community Edition, so expression-based formatting is the correct way ✔️


---

🔹 Output Example

Currency	Balance Value	Display

INR	1234.5	1,234.50
USD	1234.5	1,234.5000
EUR	10	10.0000



---

🔹 Pro Tips 💡

Always prefer BigDecimal for currency fields

Avoid pattern switching via parameters (not reliable in CE)

This works in PDF, XLS, HTML exports



